{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n38 \n \n \nquestionable data.  Yet, 102 flames were ignited with the second burner, which is nearly \n2.5 times the number of required normal-flame tests. \n \nACME\u2019s sintered spherical burners do not yield spherically symmetric flames which are \ncentered on the burner\u2019s center, where that combination is important for true spherical \nsymmetry.  This lack of symmetry is especially evident at high burner flows.  It is very likely \nthat the sintered attachment of the sphere to the supply tube is more porous than the \nsintered sphere itself because the flame is seen to be shifted toward the supply tube.  \nThere was significant improvement when the starting burner (serial number 1002) was \nreplaced mid-way through round 1 with an alternate burner (serial number 1003).  But \nthe symmetry is not perfect and this is a shortcoming of the Flame Design data. \n \nPortions of ACME\u2019s recorded image data is sometimes deleted without downlinking.  This \nis done because of operational constraints which limit the amount of data that can be \nmanaged per test day.  Given the constraint, the decision is sometimes made to sacrifice \nsome image data (e.g., from the HiBMs camera) to allow additional tests to be conducted.  \nThis is intentionally done in order to maximize the scientific return of each day of testing \nwithin the project constraints. \n \nData quality is of course dependent on the experimental hardware.  But neglecting that \nlimitation on data quality, the cameras are sensitive to radiation where pixels gradually \nbecome \u2018hot\u2019 (i.e., dead) over time.  While these bright spots are addressed in image post-\nprocessing, they are a degradation of the image quality. \n \nACME\u2019s Mass Flow Controllers (MFCs) used to control the flow of fuel and nitrogen have \nunfortunately not always performed adequately, where the flow rates have not always \nbeen appropriately steady.  The resulting effects on the flame behavior are tentatively \nassumed to be minor, but this still needs to be assessed. \n \nThe ACME camera is not of sufficient quality to demonstrate the novel aspects of the \nexperiments to the public, but it appears to be satisfactory from a science perspective. \n \nIt is possible that the tests are subtly affected by the acceleration environment, but there \nis no obvious indication of this and this has thus far not been correlated with available \nmeasurements from the Space Acceleration Measurement System (SAMS).  In this regard,", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 38}}